# Cloudflare Pages _redirects
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
#
# Same-origin proxy for the API + WebSocket. Without this, the SPA at
# rmpgutah.us would have to call https://api.rmpgutah.us/api/* directly
# (cross-origin), which means every request must pass connect-src CSP
# check against api.rmpgutah.us. The zone-level Cloudflare Transform Rule
# that injects the production CSP has, in the past, silently dropped that
# origin from connect-src — instantly breaking every API call from the
# SPA. Routing /api/* through Pages as a 200-proxy keeps requests
# same-origin so connect-src 'self' is sufficient regardless of what the
# Transform Rule looks like.
#
# Status 200 = transparent proxy (browser sees rmpgutah.us as origin).
# :splat captures everything after /api/ and is appended to the target.

/api/*  https://api.rmpgutah.us/api/:splat  200

# REMOVED 2026-07-25: four status-200 "rewrite to another origin" rules
# (/downloads/*, /updates/*, /download, /rmpg-seal.png).
#
# Cloudflare Pages supports redirect statuses (301/302/303/307/308) but NOT
# status-200 rewrites to another origin — that is a Netlify feature Pages does
# not implement. All four were silently ignored and fell through to the
# `/*  /index.html  200` catch-all below, so the request returned the app shell
# with HTTP 200. A download then saved ~11 KB of HTML under the artifact's
# filename and nothing reported an error. Measured before removal:
#
#   rmpgutah.us/downloads/<os-zip>     -> 200 text/html   (app shell)
#   rmpgutah.us/updates/latest.yml     -> 200 text/html   (app shell)
#   rmpgutah.us/download               -> 200 text/html   (app shell)
#   rmpgutah.us/rmpg-seal.png          -> 404
#   api.rmpgutah.us/updates/latest.yml -> 200 text/yaml   (works)
#
# Nothing depended on them: /downloads/* is replaced by absolute URLs from
# /api/downloads/info plus a rmpgutah.us/downloads/* zone route in
# wrangler.toml (a Worker route, which does work); electron-updater hardcodes
# https://api.rmpgutah.us/updates/ in desktop/updater.js; and root-level assets
# already 404 on live, which is why PDFs switched to Vite ?url imports (see
# client/src/utils/pdfAssets.ts).
#
# /api/* below is intentionally KEPT. It is almost certainly inert too,
# surviving only because the separate rmpg-api-proxy Worker route covers the
# same path — but if that reasoning is wrong, deleting it breaks every API call
# in the product, and removing one redundant line does not justify that risk.

# Missing build assets must 404, NOT fall through to the SPA catch-all.
# After a deploy the build emits new hashed chunk names and DELETES the old
# ones. A long-lived tab still running the previous bundle then imports an
# old hash (e.g. /assets/WarrantsPage-OLDHASH.js). Without this rule that
# path matches the `/*  /index.html  200` SPA fallback below and Pages
# returns index.html (HTTP 200, text/html) for a `.js` request. The browser
# refuses to execute HTML as an ES module ("Expected a JavaScript-or-Wasm
# module script but the server responded with a MIME type of text/html") and
# the service worker even caches that HTML under the .js URL, persisting the
# failure. Returning a real 404 instead lets the client's lazyRetry reload to
# the fresh index. Existing (valid) assets are served by Pages' static-file
# layer BEFORE _redirects rules are consulted, so this only ever fires for
# genuinely-missing hashes — never for the current build's assets.
# Must sit ABOVE the `/*` catch-all (first match wins).
/assets/*  /index.html  404

# SPA fallback — every non-asset, non-API path serves index.html so the
# React Router can handle the route client-side.
/*  /index.html  200
